WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.738
Welcome back to your Danish citizenship exam companion.

2
00:00:03.688 --> 00:00:06.936
This is Chapter Two, Episode Three, Part Two.

3
00:00:07.486 --> 00:00:11.436
Now to the third power: the courts, domstolene.

4
00:00:11.386 --> 00:00:13.274
They hold the judicial power.

5
00:00:13.274 --> 00:00:15.686
This is laid down in the constitution.

6
00:00:15.686 --> 00:00:17.811
Danish courts are independent.

7
00:00:17.811 --> 00:00:23.861
That means the Folketing and the government cannot concretely decide how the courts should judge in a trial.

8
00:00:23.861 --> 00:00:31.261
Judges are appointed on the recommendation of an independent Judicial Appointments Council, Dommerudnævnelsesråd.

9
00:00:31.261 --> 00:00:37.648
To be appointed as a Supreme Court judge, you must also first be assessed as suitable by the Supreme Court.

10
00:00:37.648 --> 00:00:42.986
And judges can, as a clear main rule, not be dismissed administratively.

11
00:00:42.986 --> 00:00:48.162
In the constitution it says that in their calling, judges have only to follow the law.

12
00:00:48.712 --> 00:00:53.825
The courts must function as impartial conflict-solvers that everyone can trust.

13
00:00:53.775 --> 00:00:56.400
A judge must appear neutral when in court.

14
00:00:56.400 --> 00:01:02.037
A judge may therefore not wear symbols that indicate a religious or political conviction.

15
00:01:02.037 --> 00:01:04.908
This is meant to support general trust in the courts.

16
00:01:05.458 --> 00:01:08.571
The courts can also control the Folketing's laws.

17
00:01:08.521 --> 00:01:12.346
The Folketing must not act in conflict with the constitution.

18
00:01:12.346 --> 00:01:17.146
That means the courts can take a stand on whether a law conflicts with the constitution.

19
00:01:17.146 --> 00:01:23.783
In that case, the courts can set the law aside, even if it has been passed by a majority in the Folketing.

20
00:01:23.783 --> 00:01:31.158
The Danish courts have, however, so far been very cautious about setting laws aside, even though it has actually happened.

21
00:01:31.158 --> 00:01:39.702
In quite a few other countries, it is more common that the courts carry out a more thorough check on whether laws comply with the country's constitution.

22
00:01:40.252 --> 00:01:44.427
The courts also control the government and the public administration.

23
00:01:44.377 --> 00:01:51.902
The courts can thus decide whether, for example, a ministry or a municipality has made an unlawful decision in a case.

24
00:01:51.902 --> 00:01:57.952
Today, citizens also have many other options if they want to complain about the public administration.

25
00:01:57.952 --> 00:02:05.515
For example, several boards have been established that supplement the courts and have the task of deciding complaint cases.

26
00:02:05.515 --> 00:02:13.224
It is also possible to complain to the Parliamentary Ombudsman, who can express criticism of the authorities' decisions or case handling.

27
00:02:13.774 --> 00:02:24.786
Finally, it is the courts' task to solve legal conflicts between citizens, and to pass judgment in criminal cases, where a citizen is accused of having committed something punishable.

28
00:02:25.336 --> 00:02:26.774
Now the structure.

29
00:02:26.724 --> 00:02:31.211
The highest court in Denmark is the Supreme Court, Højesteret.

30
00:02:31.211 --> 00:02:33.974
It has existed since 1661.

31
00:02:33.974 --> 00:02:41.849
The Supreme Court first and foremost deals with cases that have previously been handled in one of the two high courts, the landsretter.

32
00:02:41.849 --> 00:02:45.499
It is therefore said that the Supreme Court is a court of appeal.

33
00:02:45.499 --> 00:02:49.049
That means you cannot bring cases directly to the Supreme Court.

34
00:02:49.049 --> 00:02:52.956
The Supreme Court's judgments are final and cannot be appealed.

35
00:02:53.506 --> 00:02:58.369
Cases begin, as a main rule, in the city court, the byret.

36
00:02:58.319 --> 00:03:01.006
A case can normally be appealed once.

37
00:03:01.006 --> 00:03:06.281
It is said that you have the right to have your case tried in two instances, the two-instance principle.

38
00:03:06.281 --> 00:03:11.619
If a case begins in the city court, it can as a main rule be appealed to the high court.

39
00:03:11.619 --> 00:03:18.631
A case which has been appealed from the city court to the high court can be appealed to the Supreme Court with a special permission.

40
00:03:18.631 --> 00:03:26.281
The city court is the first court instance in Denmark, the high court the second, and the Supreme Court the third and last.

41
00:03:26.281 --> 00:03:30.944
There is one Supreme Court, two high courts and 24 city courts.

42
00:03:30.944 --> 00:03:41.238
In addition, there is the Maritime and Commercial Court in Copenhagen, which especially deals with cases where great knowledge of business conditions is important.

43
00:03:41.788 --> 00:03:45.288
Greenland and the Faroe Islands have their own courts.

44
00:03:45.238 --> 00:03:48.550
The Faroe Islands have the Court of the Faroe Islands.

45
00:03:48.550 --> 00:03:54.938
Greenland has four district courts, as well as the Court of Greenland and the High Court of Greenland.

46
00:03:54.938 --> 00:04:07.128
The courts in Greenland and the Faroe Islands are integrated with the Danish legal system, and their decisions can in the end be appealed to the Supreme Court, just like Danish judgments in lower courts.

47
00:04:07.678 --> 00:04:09.978
Here is your recap for Episode Three.

48
00:04:09.928 --> 00:04:14.003
The government holds the executive power and drafts most bills.

49
00:04:14.003 --> 00:04:23.265
A majority government has a majority in the Folketing; since the Second World War most governments have been minority governments, supported by support parties.

50
00:04:23.265 --> 00:04:32.953
The four oldest parties, the Social Democrats, Venstre, the Social Liberal Party and the Conservative People's Party, are the government-bearing parties.

51
00:04:32.953 --> 00:04:39.515
Since 1920, no single party has had a majority, which created the cooperating democracy.

52
00:04:39.515 --> 00:04:49.165
Prime minister years since 1901: Social Democrats 63, Venstre 37, Social Liberal Party 12, Conservatives 10.

53
00:04:49.165 --> 00:04:55.315
Hilmar Baunsgaard of the Social Liberal Party was prime minister in 1968 to 71.

54
00:04:55.315 --> 00:05:00.365
A minister can appoint one to three special advisers; civil servants are neutral.

55
00:05:00.365 --> 00:05:08.128
There have been 17 to 25 ministers in each government since 2000, and a minister need not be a member of the Folketing.

56
00:05:08.128 --> 00:05:14.640
The Folketing is elected for at most four years, and the prime minister can call an election at any time.

57
00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:27.858
The courts are independent; the Supreme Court has existed since 1661; there is one Supreme Court, two high courts and 24 city courts; and a judge may not wear religious or political symbols.

58
00:05:28.408 --> 00:05:31.008
Now it is time for your exam practice.

59
00:05:30.958 --> 00:05:35.870
These are real questions from past Danish citizenship exams on what you just heard.

60
00:05:35.870 --> 00:05:43.356
For each one, I will read the question and the choices, then pause so you can pick your answer, and then I will give you the correct answer.

61
00:05:43.906 --> 00:05:45.156
Question 1.

62
00:05:45.106 --> 00:05:48.493
May a judge wear religious symbols in a courtroom in Denmark?

63
00:05:48.493 --> 00:05:50.118
Option A: Yes.

64
00:05:50.118 --> 00:05:51.774
Option B: No.
[DA] Må en dommer bære religiøse symboler i en retssal i Danmark?  A: Ja  |  B: Nej

65
00:05:58.324 --> 00:06:00.324
The answer is B: No.

66
00:06:00.274 --> 00:06:04.536
It appeared in the June 2020 and November 2021 exams.
[DA] ✔ B: Nej

67
00:06:05.086 --> 00:06:06.336
Question 2.

68
00:06:06.286 --> 00:06:10.023
Can the government decide how a judge should rule in a specific trial?

69
00:06:10.023 --> 00:06:11.548
Option A: No.

70
00:06:11.548 --> 00:06:13.290
Option B: Yes.
[DA] Kan regeringen bestemme, hvordan en dommer skal dømme i en konkret retssag?  A: Nej  |  B: Ja

71
00:06:19.840 --> 00:06:21.515
The answer is A: No.

72
00:06:21.465 --> 00:06:25.836
It appeared in the June 2021 and June 2022 exams.
[DA] ✔ A: Nej

73
00:06:26.386 --> 00:06:27.723
Question 3.

74
00:06:27.673 --> 00:06:30.161
What is the highest court in Denmark called?

75
00:06:30.161 --> 00:06:33.086
Option A: Landsretten, the High Court.

76
00:06:33.086 --> 00:06:36.586
Option B: Højesteret, the Supreme Court.

77
00:06:36.586 --> 00:06:39.030
Option C: Statsretten.
[DA] Hvad hedder den øverste domstol i Danmark?  A: Landsretten  |  B: Højesteret  |  C: Statsretten

78
00:06:45.580 --> 00:06:49.317
The answer is B: Højesteret, the Supreme Court.

79
00:06:49.267 --> 00:06:52.176
It appeared in the November 2021 exam.
[DA] ✔ B: Højesteret

80
00:06:52.726 --> 00:06:54.026
Question 4.

81
00:06:53.976 --> 00:06:57.763
Where can a decision from the city court, byretten, be appealed?

82
00:06:57.763 --> 00:07:00.763
Option A: The High Court, Landsretten.

83
00:07:00.763 --> 00:07:04.530
Option B: The Supreme Court, Højesteret.
[DA] Hvortil kan man anke en afgørelse fra byretten?  A: Landsretten  |  B: Højesteret

84
00:07:11.080 --> 00:07:14.367
The answer is A: The High Court, Landsretten.

85
00:07:14.317 --> 00:07:18.996
It appeared in the June 2022 and November 2023 exams.
[DA] ✔ A: Landsretten

86
00:07:19.546 --> 00:07:21.308
That's the end of this part.

87
00:07:21.258 --> 00:07:22.326
Well done.

